------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase A: RDCFG latency by STALLWAIT on the Configuration Unit (C12/CFGEXU)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  BlackholeA0 STALLWAIT.md, condition C12: "Any thread has an instruction in
  any stage of the Configuration Unit pipeline", with block bit B7 (inside
  STALL_THREAD) "Block thread from starting new Configuration Unit
  instructions". BlackholeA0 RDCFG.md: "After issuing one or more `RDCFG`
  instructions, software is encouraged to use `STALLWAIT` to wait for the
  Configuration Unit to no longer be busy."
  Predicted from ConfigurationUnit.md's stage table: RDCFG holds stage 0 then
  stage +1, RMWCIB holds stage 0 alone, so the difference is >= 1 cycle/pair.

  variant thr        rdcfg      rmwcib      setdma   d(rmwcib)   d(setdma)
  t1      1          2.968       2.968       2.968      +0.000      +0.000

  bare occupancies (must agree, or the difference is occupancy):
    RDCFG 0.998   RMWCIB0 0.998   SETDMAREG 0.998

TTBENCH_CFGLAT_OCC: 0.9979 0.9978 0.9978
TTBENCH_CFGLAT_STALLCOST: 1.9702
TTBENCH_CFGLAT_COND: C12 CFGEXU 0x1000
TTBENCH_CFGLAT_PAIRS: 2.9680 2.9679 2.9680
TTBENCH_CFGLAT_DIFF: 0.0001 0.0000

  DIFFERENCE 0.000, BELOW THE 0.5 CYCLE/PAIR FLOOR against both baselines.
  ConfigurationUnit.md predicts >= 1, so this is the NULL and not a small
  value. EXPECTED against tt-sim, which reads Blackhole's STALLWAIT
  condition mask as 12 bits (raw 11:0, tt_sim/pe/tensix/backends/sync.py
  `_read_wait_res`) where the ISA doc gives 13, so C12 never survives the
  decode and the wait degrades to the 0x7F `all resources` fallback. On a
  card it means the construction did not reach the quantity.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase A: the wrong-condition control (slots 20/21, TRISC_CFG)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  variant thr    rdcfg/pair    base/pair   difference
  t1      1           2.968        2.968       +0.000

TTBENCH_CFGLAT_WRONGBIT: 0.0001
  TRISC_CFG is about the RISCV core's outstanding memory requests, not
  about the Configuration Unit's pipeline, so ~0 here is the CORRECT
  reading and is what makes the C12 difference above attributable to the
  condition bit. A control that moved would mean it is not.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase A: RDCFG -> GPR -> consumer, dependent against independent (26/27)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Both arms are TTI_RDCFG(60,0) followed by TTI_ADDDMAREG(1,63,0,X).
  X = 60 in slot 26 (the RDCFG destination) and X = 59 in slot 27.
  BlackholeA0 RDCFG.md makes the separation software's job, so the
  documented prediction is that this difference is ZERO and the latency
  is not a duration at all. See the visibility section below.

  variant thr      dep/pair   indep/pair   difference
  t1      1           2.968        2.968       +0.000

TTBENCH_DEP_DIFF: 0.0001
TTBENCH_DEP_PARTS: 2.9682 0.9979 2.9723
  the dependent pair costs 2.968 cycles against 0.998 + 2.972 = 3.970 for
  the two instructions measured bare (probes 14 and 10). A pair that
  came in UNDER the sum would mean an arm is not issuing both
  instructions, and then a zero difference would say nothing.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase A: RDCFG's latency as a producer-to-consumer DISTANCE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  seeds 0xA5A5A5A5 / 0x5A5A5A5A, never-ran marker 0xDEADBEEF, config index 0,
  64 repetitions per separation.

  control: no RDCFG at all  -> 0xA5A5A5A5, 0x5A5A5A5A (want 0xA5A5A5A5, 0x5A5A5A5A)  ok
  control: RDCFG at d=8     -> 0x00000000, 0x00000000 (want them equal, and
                               neither seed nor marker)                ok
  consumer never ran: 0 repetitions;  unexplained: 0

  d         fresh      stale      other
  1            64          0          0
  2            64          0          0
  3            64          0          0
  4            64          0          0

TTBENCH_VIS_CONTROLS: stale-ok fresh-ok 0 0
TTBENCH_VIS_DMIN: 1 64
TTBENCH_VIS_COUNTS: 64 64 64 64
  d_min is the smallest separation at which EVERY repetition saw the value
  RDCFG wrote. Because the consumer may read its operand some cycles into
  its own execution, d_min is a LOWER BOUND on the latency -- which is the
  direction the charging policy takes bounds in. d_min = 2 corroborates
  ConfigurationUnit.md's '>= 2 cycles' exactly; d_min = 1 would leave the
  '>= 2' unreached rather than refute it, since a consumer that reads late
  is a complete alternative explanation. A MIXTURE at any d (fresh and
  stale both non-zero) means the RISC-V front end did not deliver the
  sequence at one instruction per cycle and the separation is not what it
  says; that is why every repetition is counted rather than one taken.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase A: is C12 live? the cross-thread control (slots 28/29)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  thread 1 runs STALLWAIT(STALL_THREAD, CFGEXU)+NOP in slot 28 and NOP+NOP
  in slot 29; threads 0 and 2 run RMWCIB0+NOP in BOTH, so at t2/t3 they
  hold the Configuration Unit from outside thread 1's issue path. C12 is
  "Any thread has an instruction in any stage of the Configuration Unit
  pipeline", so a live C12 must make thread 1's stall grow with the
  number of hammering threads. Slot 29 carries the cross-thread issue
  interference that grows anyway, and subtracts it.

  variant thr    stall/pair    null/pair d = difference
  t1      1           2.968        1.996       +0.972

  needs t1 AND one of t2/t3 in the same run to grade; got t1 only.
  Run with --variants t1,t3.

